{
  "index_test": "mixed-animal-and-nonanimal",
  "index_test_evidence": "The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) and European Medicines Agency generally require the safety of new drug candidates to be evaluated in both rodent and nonrodent animal models, frequently rat and dog, before moving the new chemical entity into human clinical trials. An analysis of 150 drugs that caused adverse events in humans found that regulatory testing in rats and dogs correctly predicted just 71% of toxicities in humans (1).",
  "decision": "include",
  "reason": "The study uses both whole animal (rat and dog) data and in vitro (Liver-Chip) data to predict human clinical outcomes. The inclusion criteria state that studies evaluating both whole-animal in vivo results and non-animal methods against human outcomes should be included."
}
